A||N||5||Iron Duke Wellingsley||Virtual Dyno Users:  How power from this Duke build?||||5||
Z||000000||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-09-2001||03:12 AM||||Hi,<P>I am about to assemble an Iron Duke engine and I wondered what to expect from it.  I have seen virtual dyno runs on the camshaft I chose, but not with a Holley TBI and manifold.<P>Here's the build:<BR>0.020" overbore hypereutectic 9:1 compression pistons/moly rings<BR>3 angle vavle job<BR>deck head and block 0.010" each<BR>Holley 2" bore TBI and Dominator manifold<BR>MSD 6AL ignition, Accel coil and wires<BR>Aftermarket catalytic converter with stock muffler<P>And the camshaft (with compucam lifters)<P>Compucam Pontiac High energy camshafts, Hydraulic--Good combination of torque and power. Performance street and marine.<BR>operating rpm range : 1200-5200<BR>duration in degrees : advertised in.:260 advertised ex.:260 @.050" in.:212 @.050" ex.:212<BR>lift w/ rocker arms :1.75 rocker arms in:.489", ex:.489"<BR>lobe sep. angle : 110°<P>A previous post listed this cam as 108HP and 144 ft/lbs torque on a stock engine.  <P><BR>Before the build, my engine showed signs of valvetrain problems (periodic drop in idle vacuum, &gt;100 rpm idle variance).  The car is an automatic and it had a sloppy CV joint at the time.  <P>The car shook pretty hard on the dyno.  It achieved a very sad 68 HP at 3500 rpm.  The air fuel mixture was very rich, especially past 3500 rpm.  Fuel pressure was set at 12.5 psi for the test and all the sensors were new.<P>Along with the engine rebuild, I have fixed the CV problems, installed new motor mounts and cradle bushings, etc.  My target is 90 HP at the wheel when I return to the dyno in June. <P>Does that seem reasonable? <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Taijiguy||05-09-2001||08:38 AM||||I'm trying to run your simulation, but I need to know the motor year- 84-88 had 9:1 compression, except 87 which had 8.5:1. Valve dimnsions (can't find 'em anyplace) and also the CFM rating of the throttle body (and manifold if you have it) Any other info such as porting, etc. would be helpful as well. The more info I have, the more accurate the results. Any dreams you have, include those as well, since they can be compared.||||reg||4
Z||000002||88formula||05-09-2001||05:47 PM||||I assume you have the Competition Cams 14-123-4 hydraulic camshaft. If you do than your engine will make about 111 horsepower @ 4500 rpm and 146 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm and a peak of 147-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm.<P> If you have an automatic with the 3.18 final drive than you car will go 0-60 mph in 10.2 seconds and run the quarter mile in 17.8 seconds @ 75.8 mph.  If you have the 84 fiero with the four speed manual transmission than you car will run 0-60 mph in 9.8 seconds and run the quarter mile in 17 seconds @ 78.8 mph. If you have the five speed transmission than your car will go 0-60 mph in 9.3 seconds and run the quarter mile in 16.87 seconds @ 79.6 mph. I also assumed you have a 4500 rpm reline. If you don’t than your times will be slightly quicker.  <P>I don’t know about older iron dukes but if your engine has positive stop rocker arm bolts than you may have problems with valve adjustment after milling your head and block a total of .020”. When you deck the block you want the quench area to have .040”. This is the distance between the piston @ TDC and the flat part of the combustion chamber on the cylinder head. The greater this distance is from that .040” benchmark the more likely you are going to have problems with detonation or abnormal combustion. Deck the block so you have .005” from the top of the piston to the top of the deck. That distance plus .035” for the head gasket will give you the desirable .040” quench area. You should also dull any sharp edges in the combustion chambers so they can’t promote pre-ignition, which leads to detonation. You also should have the reciprocating assembly balanced for the best possible longest lasting and running engine. <P>Your car will put down about 88.8 horsepower to the rear wheels with a 20% drive line loss.       <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||05-09-2001||06:21 PM||||Here is some info for those interested||||reg||14
Z||000004||84Bill||05-09-2001||06:21 PM||||Here is some info for those interested<P>A LOT OF INFO HERE<BR> <A HREF="http://dukemods.50megs.com/WEB%20PAGE/PAGE1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://dukemods.50megs.com/WEB%20PAGE/PAGE1.html</A> <P>And My site I'll be building a souped up duke you can watch the progress here <A HREF="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-09-2001||09:58 PM||||The car is an 84 automatic, so I think that I have the answers I was looking for.  The engine is not ported (other than the 3 angle valve job).  <P>I should expect about 85 HP at the wheel as my rebuilt duke is good for about 110HP.<P>That sounds great to me.  I actually was surprised that it only had 68HP on the first dyno run.<P>Going from 68 to 88 is about a 25% increase, so somehow I suspect I will notice the difference  <P>Not hearing the bad valve noises the engine made before would be enough to make me happy.  I am be replacing the cam, lifters, pushrods, rockers, and valve springs to be really sure the valve noises go away.<P>Thanks for the help!||||reg||1
